      "text": "Data and migrations: not applicable. There is no database or schema. The case log is a new file in a new case folder, with nothing to backfill. Rollback is not running `cma case check` or reinstalling the previous wheel, because nothing else reads log.jsonl yet.",
      "proof_cmd": "git diff --stat 576e43d..HEAD -- src",
      "proof_output": " src/cma/case.py | 559 +++...\n src/cma/cli.py  |  22 +++\n 2 files changed, 581 insertions(+)"
    },
    {
      "text": "Performance: not applicable to a hot path. verify is linear in the number of entries, and each append rewrites a head that holds every hash, which is O(n) per append. No timing is claimed because no configured tier measures it.",
      "proof_cmd": "none (no runner tier measures timing)",
      "proof_output": "not claimed"
    },
    {
      "text": "Observability, operability and docs: `cma case check` prints the intact entry count and the head hash, or exits 3 naming the first bad entry. That non-zero exit is the local alert, as the design specifies ('no remote alerting, by design'). The README documents the command. Under 'gap' in notes: the design's structured per-event log line (event=… counts) and a changelog line.",
      "proof_cmd": "grep -n 'case check' README.md",
      "proof_output": "56:cma case check private/cma/cases/<id>"
    }
  ],
  "files_changed": [
    "tests/test_case_log.py"
  ],
  "dispositions": [
    {
      "finding": "major src/cma/case.py:296: the integrity check is unreachable from production case loading.",
      "disposition": "not_reproduced",
      "evidence": "At HEAD f4c9c05, src/cma/cli.py registers `cma case check`, which calls case.for_case(folder).verify() and exits 3 on a broken log. The invariants tier runs test_p15_loading_a_case_detects_a_tampered_log_and_names_the_first_bad_entry[change|remove|reorder|rebuild] and the torn-entry variant through the Typer app, and all of them PASS (82 passed). This round adds the exit-code contract tests. Opening a case in the workspace and sign-off remain T13 and T27, which must call require_intact."
    },
    {
      "finding": "major src/cma/case.py:322: a reader can report a healthy log as corrupted during rapid appends when the lock is contended after the retries run out.",
      "disposition": "not_reproduced",
      "evidence": "At HEAD, case.py:348 returns check.as_recorded() when the first failure lies after every entry the head file records. test_a_reader_never_reports_a_healthy_log_broken_while_appends_keep_landing reproduces the reviewer's scenario exactly: two commits on each of READ_RETRIES attempts and the lock held at the fallback. It PASSES with ok, in_progress and a contiguous seq. I also probed for a masking regression: with the head file deleted and the lock held, a reader still reports 'head file … missing' at entry 1, because a stable non-torn failure returns before the fallback."
    }
  ],
  "notes": "Gaps that need the owner: (1) The design's structured per-event log line (event=… traced=… with no names) is not emitted; it belongs with the case store and pack events (T13 and T27). (2) There is no changelog file in the repo; a release note line belongs with the case-store release. (3) The head file holding every hash makes each append O(n). This is fine at case scale, but if R52-scale logs appear, a design change (for example, chunked or Merkle heads) is needed; no timing is claimed. (4) T13 and T27 must call require_intact when they open a case and at sign-off; `cma case check` is the only production caller today. (5) Rewriting the log and the head file together is undetectable by design; the sign-off and pack pin the head. I tried to confirm the keep_landing test fails without the fix by temporarily editing case.py, but the permission layer denied the command, so case.py is unchanged and that negative check is not done here."
}

